WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.047
This slide summarizes the four main row-preservation patterns.

00:00:04.047 --> 00:00:06.642
INNER JOIN keeps only matches.

00:00:06.642 --> 00:00:12.692
If a patient has no admission, that patient will not appear in a patient-to-admission INNER JOIN.

00:00:12.692 --> 00:00:17.932
This is useful when the question is specifically about records that exist in both tables.

00:00:17.932 --> 00:00:21.207
LEFT JOIN keeps every row from the left table.

00:00:21.207 --> 00:00:26.067
If there is no matching row on the right, the right-side columns become NULL.

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:29.522
RIGHT JOIN does the same thing in the opposite direction.

00:00:29.522 --> 00:00:33.532
FULL OUTER JOIN keeps unmatched rows from both sides.

00:00:33.532 --> 00:00:38.452
Try to connect each join type to a real question.

00:00:38.452 --> 00:00:42.172
In education, INNER JOIN could show only students who submitted an assignment.

00:00:42.172 --> 00:00:46.637
LEFT JOIN could show every student and their submission when one exists.

00:00:46.637 --> 00:00:53.462
FULL OUTER JOIN could compare two class rosters and reveal students who appear in only one system.

00:00:53.462 --> 00:00:56.857
Do not rely only on Venn-diagram-style pictures.

00:00:56.857 --> 00:01:03.522
Those pictures can help, but the stronger habit is to ask, what population must remain complete?

00:01:03.522 --> 00:01:08.102
If the answer is every patient, put patients on the preserved side.

00:01:08.102 --> 00:01:11.837
If the answer is every department, preserve departments.

00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:18.160
If the answer is records from both systems, even unmatched ones, think about FULL OUTER JOIN.
